        <div id="what-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Is a Single-Stage Snow Blower? One Auger Does Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            A single-stage snow blower uses one spinning auger to do two jobs at once: it scoops snow into the housing and flings it straight out of the discharge chute. There is no separate impeller, which is what makes these machines lighter, smaller and cheaper than two-stage models. For a typical paved driveway in a region that gets storms of a few inches at a time, a single-stage is often all the machine you need.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How the Auger Works</h3>

          <p>
            The auger spins fast, usually with rubber paddles or a steel blade, and its shape pushes snow toward the center of the housing and up the chute in one motion. Because the auger does all the throwing, throw distance depends on auger speed and how much snow is packed into it. That is why single-stage machines throw farthest in dry, fluffy snow and slow down in wet, heavy snow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Auger-Assisted Drive</h3>

          <p>
            On most single-stage machines the bottom of the auger touches the ground. As it spins, it grips the pavement and pulls the machine forward, so you push less than you would expect. This is called auger-assisted drive or auger propulsion. It is not true self-propulsion with powered wheels, but on a flat paved driveway it makes a 50 to 100 pound machine feel easy to move.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Cannot Do</h3>

          <p>
            The same design creates the limits. An auger that throws snow directly cannot handle the depth a two-stage can, and an auger that touches the ground will pick up anything loose on the surface. Those two facts, snow depth and surface type, decide whether a single-stage is right for you, and the rest of this guide explains both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="single-vs-two" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Single-Stage vs Two-Stage: Choose by Snow Depth and Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single stage vs dual stage question comes down to four things: how deep your snow gets, what your driveway is made of, how big it is, and how much machine you want to store and push.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Snow Depth</h3>

          <p>
            Single-stage machines are at their best in snow up to about 6 to 8 inches, and their intake heights run from about 10 to 13 inches. Two-stage machines have taller housings of 19 to 21 inches and a separate impeller, so they handle deep storms and the dense snow a plow leaves behind far more easily. If your typical storm is a foot or more, buy a two-stage; our <a href="/best-gas-snow-blower/">best gas snow blowers guide</a> ranks them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Driveway Surface</h3>

          <p>
            Single-stage augers touch the ground, which is perfect for scraping pavement clean and a problem on gravel. Two-stage housings ride on adjustable skid shoes above the surface, so they work on gravel when the shoes are raised. One battery pick here, the EGO SNT2122, adds adjustable skid shoes to a single-stage design, which makes it the exception.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Driveway Size and Storage</h3>

          <p>
            For a one- or two-car driveway, walks and a patio, a single-stage is quicker to get out, easier to turn and small enough to hang on a wall. For a long or wide driveway, a two-stage's wider path and self-propelled drive save real time. Single-stage machines are also far lighter, which matters if you have steps to the garage or limited strength.
          </p>

