        <div id="gravel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Paved vs Gravel Driveways: The Auger Touches the Ground
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the most important question to answer before buying a single-stage snow blower. Can I use a single stage snow blower on gravel? Usually not, and it is worth understanding why.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Single-Stage Machines Suit Pavement</h3>

          <p>
            On asphalt, concrete and pavers, the ground-contact auger is an advantage. Rubber paddles scrape down to the surface and leave it almost bare, which means less ice forming later and less salt needed. A two-stage, riding on skid shoes, usually leaves a thin layer behind. For a paved driveway, a single-stage often gives a cleaner result.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Happens on Gravel</h3>

          <p>
            On gravel, the same auger scoops up stones along with the snow and throws them out of the chute at speed. That can break windows, chip car paint, dent siding and injure anyone nearby. It also wears out rubber paddles and can damage the auger and chute. Most makers tell owners not to use a standard single-stage on gravel, and you should follow that instruction.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Skid Shoe Exception</h3>

          <p>
            A few newer single-stage machines add adjustable skid shoes that lift the housing slightly off the ground. EGO lists them on the SNT2122 for gravel driveways and uneven terrain. Set high, they leave a thin layer of snow over the stones, and that layer protects them. Even then, go slowly, keep people and cars clear of the chute, and wait until a base of packed snow or frozen ground has formed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Better Options for Gravel</h3>

          <p>
            If your driveway is all gravel, a two-stage machine with skid shoes set high is the safer and more effective choice. Our <a href="/best-snow-blower/">best snow blowers guide</a> covers two-stage and track-drive models. Many people with gravel drives also keep a single-stage for paved walks and a patio, where it excels.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="snow-depth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Snow Can a Single-Stage Handle? About 6 to 8 Inches Comfortably
          </h2>

          <p>
            Listings quote clearing depths of 10 to 13 inches, but that is the height of the housing opening, not the depth of snow the machine clears comfortably in one pass.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Intake Height vs Real Depth</h3>

          <p>
            Intake height tells you the tallest wall of snow the housing can take in. In practice, single-stage machines work best when the snow is well below that figure. In 6 to 8 inches of fresh, dry snow, a good single-stage moves at walking pace. Near its intake height, it slows, clogs more often and throws a shorter distance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wet and Heavy Snow</h3>

          <p>
            Wet snow is far heavier than powder for the same depth, and it is the hardest test for a single-stage. Slow down, take narrower passes so the auger is not fully loaded, and keep the chute clear. Machines with steel augers and more power, like the EGO models and the Briggs 223cc, cope with it better than small machines.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clear in Stages During a Big Storm</h3>

          <p>
            When a storm will drop more than your machine can handle, go out partway through rather than waiting for it to end. Clearing 6 inches twice is quicker and easier on the machine than clearing 12 inches once. It also prevents the bottom layer from compacting into ice under foot traffic and tires.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plow-berm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Plow Berm: A Single-Stage's Hardest Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            The ridge of packed, icy snow a street plow leaves across the end of your driveway is the job that defeats most single-stage machines. It is dense, often chunky with ice, and can be taller than the housing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Berm Is So Hard</h3>

          <p>
            Plowed snow has been compressed and often mixed with slush and road salt, then frozen. A rubber paddle auger tends to ride up and bounce off it rather than cutting in. Steel augers, like those on the EGO SNT2122 and SNT2114, are designed to bite into exactly this kind of snow, which is why EGO mentions the plow in its listings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Clear It</h3>

          <p>
            Attack the berm in thin slices rather than driving straight in. Raise the front of the machine slightly by pressing down on the handle, take a few inches off the top, then lower it and take the next layer. Clear it as soon as the plow passes, before it freezes solid, and work from the side rather than head-on when you can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Use a Shovel</h3>

          <p>
            If the berm is taller than your machine's intake or frozen into ice, break it up with a shovel or ice chopper first and let the snow blower clear the loose pieces. Forcing a single-stage into a solid ridge strains belts and paddles. A good shovel is still worth owning; see our <a href="/best-snow-shovel/">best snow shovels</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-vs-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Gas or Battery Single-Stage? Runtime, Cold and Maintenance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Single-stage machines are where battery power has caught up most with gas, because the job asks for less power than a two-stage. The right choice depends on your driveway size, storms and patience for maintenance. Our <a href="/electric-vs-gas-snow-blower/">electric vs gas snow blower comparison</a> covers the wider picture.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas: Unlimited Runtime, More Upkeep</h3>

          <p>
            A gas single-stage runs as long as you have fuel, which suits long driveways and big storms. The trade-off is upkeep: fresh fuel with stabilizer, oil changes, spark plugs and careful summer storage. Electric start, like the Briggs 223cc's, removes the hardest part of cold-weather starting, but a small recoil-only engine like the NOVUS still means pulling a cord.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery: Push-Button Start, Limited Charge</h3>

          <p>
            Battery machines start instantly, run quietly enough to clear before dawn without waking the street, and need almost no maintenance. The limit is runtime. Makers quote coverage as a number of car spaces in a given snow depth, and EGO's SNT2114 kit leads here at up to a 16-car driveway in 8 inches. For most one- and two-car driveways, a two-battery kit is enough. Our <a href="/best-electric-snow-blower/">best electric snow blowers guide</a> includes corded models too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold Weather and Batteries</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium batteries lose output when cold. Store them indoors at room temperature and put them in the machine just before you go out, as Greenworks advises in its snow thrower listings. Gas engines have their own cold problems, mainly hard starts and stale fuel, which electric start and stabilizer solve.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            Choose battery if your driveway is short to medium, your storms are moderate and you value quiet and no maintenance. Choose gas if your driveway is long, storms are frequent and heavy, or you want to buy one machine and never think about charge levels.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Width Single-Stage Snow Blower Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Single-stage machines come in a narrow range of widths, from about 18 to 22 inches, but those few inches change how many passes you make.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">18 Inches: Walks and Small Spaces</h3>

          <p>
            An 18-inch machine like the NOVUS suits sidewalks, decks, patios and short single-car driveways. It is the lightest and easiest to maneuver and store, but it takes more passes on a full driveway.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">20 to 22 Inches: Most Driveways</h3>

          <p>
            A 20- to 22-inch machine is the sweet spot for one- and two-car driveways. On a 20-foot-wide driveway, a 22-inch machine needs roughly 11 passes against about 14 for an 18-inch model, accounting for overlap. The Greenworks Pro 60V, Snow Joe and Briggs are 22 inches; the EGO models are 21.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reading Driveway-Per-Charge Claims</h3>

          <p>
            Battery makers describe runtime in car spaces at a stated snow depth. Compare like with like: EGO quotes 8 inches, Worx quotes 6 inches, and some listings give no figure at all. Treat these as maker claims rather than guarantees, since wet snow and cold batteries shorten them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="toro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Toro Single-Stage Snow Blowers: Power Clear and SnowMaster
          </h2>

          <p>
            Toro is the most searched name in single-stage snow blowers, and its Power Clear gas machines and 60V battery models have a strong reputation. They are not in this ranking because, when we searched Amazon for this guide, new Toro single-stage machines were not listed there, only replacement paddles, carburetors and covers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Toro Single-Stage Models Are Sold</h3>

          <p>
            Toro sells its snow blowers mainly through home centers, hardware stores and authorized dealers. A dealer can assemble and service the machine, which is useful under warranty. Our <a href="/best-snow-blower/">best snow blowers guide</a> covers the Toro Power Clear 721 R-C, and our <a href="/toro-electric-snow-blower/">Toro electric snow blower guide</a> covers its battery models.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">SnowMaster vs Power Clear</h3>

          <p>
            Toro's SnowMaster line uses a taller single-stage design with a self-propelled drive and sits between a classic single-stage and a two-stage in size and price. Power Clear is the classic lightweight single-stage. If you are comparing them with the picks here, look at the same specs: width, intake height, engine or battery, start type and surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="self-propelled" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Do Single-Stage Snow Blowers Self-Propel?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most do not, at least not with powered wheels. They rely on the auger touching the ground to pull them forward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Auger Propulsion</h3>

          <p>
            None of the seven listings here describes a powered wheel drive, and the Briggs lists auger-assisted drive by name. On flat pavement this is enough to make pushing easy, and it means fewer parts to maintain. It also means the machine moves slowly on a hill or when the auger is lifted off the ground.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When You Need Real Self-Propulsion</h3>

          <p>
            If your driveway is steep, long, or you find pushing difficult, consider a self-propelled machine with a wheel or track drive. Our <a href="/self-propelled-snow-blower/">self-propelled snow blower guide</a> explains the options, most of which are two-stage models.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. How to Use a Single-Stage Snow Blower Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            A little technique gets far more out of a single-stage machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Plan Your Throw</h3>

          <p>
            Check the wind and decide where snow will go before you start. Throw downwind and away from the area you still need to clear, so you are not moving the same snow twice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Start in the Middle</h3>

          <p>
            On a driveway, start with a pass down the center, then work outward, throwing snow to the sides. Overlap each pass by a few inches so you do not leave ridges.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Keep the Auger Loaded, Not Buried</h3>

          <p>
            Walk at a pace that keeps a steady stream from the chute. If the throw weakens or the machine bogs down, slow down or take a narrower pass.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Finish the Edges</h3>

          <p>
            Clear along the garage door, walkways and the street end last, and tackle the plow berm in thin slices.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Single-Stage Snow Blower Safety Rules
          </h2>

          <p>
            Single-stage augers spin fast and close to the ground, which makes a few rules non-negotiable. Read and follow the maker's manual for your machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Clear a Clog by Hand</h3>

          <p>
            Turn the machine off, remove the key or battery, wait for the auger to stop and use a clearing tool or stick. Hand injuries from clogged chutes are among the most common snow blower injuries, and an auger can move after the engine stops.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch the Chute and the Surface</h3>

          <p>
            Never aim the chute at people, pets, cars or windows. Walk the area first and remove newspapers, toys, doormats, hoses and stones, which the auger will throw.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Safety</h3>

          <p>
            Run gas machines outdoors only, never in a closed garage, because of carbon monoxide. Refuel outside with the engine off and cool, and store fuel in an approved can away from flames. See our <a href="/best-gas-can/">gas can guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Paddles, Scraper Bars and Off-Season Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Single-stage machines need little maintenance, but the parts that touch the ground wear out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rubber Paddles and Scraper Bar</h3>

          <p>
            Rubber paddles wear down from contact with the pavement. When they no longer reach the ground, the machine stops scraping clean and pulls forward less. The scraper bar under the housing wears too. Both are inexpensive replacement parts; check them at the start of each season.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Engine Care</h3>

          <p>
            Change the oil as the maker recommends, check the spark plug, and use fresh fuel with stabilizer. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-prepare-a-snow-blower-for-winter/">how to prepare a snow blower for winter</a> walks through the checklist.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Storage</h3>

          <p>
            Store batteries indoors, partly charged, away from heat and extreme cold during summer. Charge them fully the day before the first forecast storm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Rubber-Paddle Machine on Gravel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The auger will throw stones at speed. Use a single-stage only on paved surfaces unless it has adjustable skid shoes, and even then keep people and cars clear.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting Until a Big Storm Ends</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single-stage handles 6 inches far better than 12. Clear partway through a heavy storm instead of waiting until the snow is deeper than the housing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving Straight Into the Plow Berm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ramming a frozen ridge strains belts and paddles. Take it in thin slices, clear it before it freezes, or break it up with a shovel first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Runtime Claims at Different Depths</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                EGO quotes car spaces in 8 inches of snow, Worx in 6 inches, and some listings give nothing. Compare like with like, and expect less in wet snow or cold.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Clearing a Clogged Chute by Hand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Always switch off, remove the key or battery, wait for the auger to stop and use a clearing tool. Never put your hand in the chute.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

