        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best single stage snow blower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most driveways, the EGO Power+ SNT2122 is the best single-stage snow blower. EGO lists a steel auger, a 21-inch path, a 42-foot throw and adjustable skid shoes for gravel. For gas, the Briggs & Stratton 22-inch 223cc with electric start is the best single-stage on Amazon, and the EGO SNT2114 suits long driveways.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are single-stage snow blowers good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for the right job. Single-stage snow blowers are light, affordable and scrape paved surfaces cleaner than most two-stage machines. They work best in snow up to about 6 to 8 inches on paved driveways and walks. For frequent deep storms, heavy plow berms or gravel, a two-stage machine is the better tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a single stage snow blower on gravel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Single-stage augers touch the ground and throw stones, which can break windows and injure people. Most makers advise against it. A few models, like the EGO SNT2122, add adjustable skid shoes that lift the housing for gravel. Otherwise, choose a two-stage with skid shoes set high, and clear the area of loose stones first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep of snow can a single stage snow blower handle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most single-stage machines have intake heights of 10 to 13 inches, but they work best in about 6 to 8 inches of snow. In deeper or wet snow, take narrower passes and go slowly, or clear partway through the storm. Snow regularly over a foot calls for a two-stage machine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between single stage and two stage snow blowers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A single-stage uses one auger to scoop and throw snow, and the auger touches the ground. A two-stage uses a slow auger to feed a separate high-speed impeller, rides on skid shoes, has a taller intake and is usually self-propelled. Two-stage machines handle deeper snow and gravel; single-stage machines are lighter and cheaper.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a battery single stage snow blower as good as gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most one- and two-car driveways, yes. Battery machines like the EGO SNT2122 start instantly, run quietly and need little maintenance, and EGO lists steel augers that handle plow berms. Gas still wins on runtime for long driveways and on cost per hour, and it does not lose output in the cold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do single stage snow blowers pull themselves?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most use auger-assisted drive: the auger touches the pavement and pulls the machine forward as it spins. That makes pushing easy on flat ground, but it is not powered-wheel self-propulsion. On steep or long driveways, or if pushing is hard for you, a self-propelled machine with a wheel or track drive is easier to use. Check the listing: a powered drive is always named.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

